  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🧚‍♀️ Fairy Town Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Homes under the hill, hollows in the wood, and the courts you should never visit: Foxgloveknowe, the Court of Moonlit Thorns."
    },
    "intro": "In folklore, fairies don't live in towns so much as in places: inside a green hill, in a ring of mushrooms, in the roots of a hawthorn nobody dares cut down. This **fairy town name generator** builds names for those places from the old words for them, and for the great courts of the fae.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Where fairies live",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Under the hill.</strong> In Irish and Scottish folklore, the fairy folk live inside mounds and hills, the <em>sídhe</em>. Scots has a word for such a hill: a <em>knowe</em>. English has <em>howe</em> and <em>barrow</em> for old burial mounds, often said to be fairy homes.</li><li><strong>Fairy rings.</strong> Circles of mushrooms in the grass were said to be where fairies danced. Step inside, and you might dance for a hundred years.</li><li><strong>Fairy trees.</strong> Lone hawthorns in Ireland are still left uncut for fear of offending the fairies who live there.</li><li><strong>Fairy forts.</strong> The ancient ringforts that dot the Irish countryside were long believed to belong to the fairies, and left alone.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Four kinds of place",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Under the hill</strong>: names ending in <em>-knowe</em>, <em>-howe</em>, <em>-barrow</em> and <em>-tump</em> (a West Country word for a mound).</li><li><strong>In the wood</strong>: <em>-hollow</em>, <em>-dell</em>, <em>-shaw</em> (a small wood) and <em>-bower</em>.</li><li><strong>Fairy ring</strong>: <em>-ring</em>, <em>-mote</em> (a mound, or a meeting place), <em>-green</em> and <em>-lea</em>.</li><li><strong>A fairy court</strong>: the grand halls of the Seelie and Unseelie Courts, named like titles: <em>the Court of Weeping Willows</em>.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Visiting fairyland",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Time runs differently.</strong> In the Irish tale of Oisín, a few years in Tír na nÓg, the Land of Youth, turn out to be three hundred in the mortal world.</li><li><strong>Don't eat the food.</strong> Eat or drink in fairyland and you may never leave: a rule from ballads like <em>Thomas the Rhymer</em> to modern fantasy.</li><li><strong>Every place needs its people.</strong> For the fairies who live there, try the <a href=\"fairy-name-generator.html\">Fairy Name Generator</a>.</li></ul>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What is a fairy knowe?",
        "answer": "A knowe is a Scots word for a small hill or mound. Fairy knowes were hills believed to be fairy dwellings, and the word gives fairy places an old, northern sound."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I use these names in D&D?",
        "answer": "Yes, they're free to use and you don't need to credit the site. They suit villages and courts in D&D's Feywild as well as folklore-based stories."
      }
    ]
  }
